<Layout>
  <h1>Philosophies</h1>

  <h2>Amito.Buddhism</h2>
  <p>
    It all started in the north of India, Himachal Pradesh, Dharamshala. Sitting
    just at the beginning of the Himalayan mountains. I was having very chill
    weeks there. Sitting in restaurants and cafes. Reading, writing, laughing,
    crying all by myself sitting on the floor and eating with my hands.
    Dharamshala is a very holy place for the Tibetan Buddhists. The Dalai Lama
    the supreme leader of the Tibetan Buddhists lives there and I was very lucky
    to have a chance to see him. I woke up that day, went to the temple saw him
    with all the ceremonies and later that day started a 10 day Tibetan Buddhism
    retreat. No phones/ technology, no talking (almost) ~3 of meditation every
    day, 5 hours of Buddhist teachings every day. In those 10 days I felt the
    clearness of my mind very vibrantly, I was having very deep realizations and
    Insights about myself and the world. Since then I've been pretty committed
    to daily meditations and started using the buddhist tools taught from the
    Dharma very efficiently and they were very efficient in finding calmness,
    focus, happiness, and clarity in my daily life.
  </p>

  <h2>Balanced Ambition</h2>
  <p>
    I want to use my skills and strengnths to do big stuff. To express myself
    and to help others. It's easy for me to start working about something, and
    quickly feel stressed and stiff. I call it toxic hyperfocus. Seems like it's
    something like adrenaline. I tought myslef that this is the way to be
    focused and be productive, assumjng it's fine to feel that horrible stress
    in my body. Then I understood that it's a spectrum: Unfocused Flow Toxic
    Hyperfocus I easily get into toxic hyperfocus, I can even feel it right now
    when I'm writing this. The main two things I remind myself are: Be Royal - I
    deserve the best, I want to service myself the best I can. stressing myself
    out is neither pleasureable or useful. See Badolina for more Observe - see
    your body and brain experiencing this. In other words your mind observing
    your experience. In other words meditation. See Amito.Buddhism for more
  </p>

  <h2>Meaning</h2>
  <p>
    For me it's all about being happy and doing good. Then, comes the challange
    of wanting to do good for others but getting burnt out and unhappy from
    that. See Balanced Ambition for more.
  </p>
</Layout>
